WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.832
Reranking can sometimes improve retrieval without adding another agent loop.

00:00:04.832 --> 00:00:08.237
A vector search might return ten candidates.

00:00:08.237 --> 00:00:12.237
The relevant chunk is present, but it is ranked seventh.

00:00:12.237 --> 00:00:16.964
A reranker can reorder those candidates and place the best three at the top.

00:00:17.464 --> 00:00:23.226
That is different from a retrieval failure where the needed evidence was never in the candidate set.

00:00:23.226 --> 00:00:28.751
If the correct chunk was retrieved but ranked poorly, reranking is a good tool.

00:00:28.751 --> 00:00:36.656
If the correct evidence was never retrieved, you may need query rewriting, another source, a different filter,

00:00:36.656 --> 00:00:38.591
or a different embedding strategy.

00:00:39.130 --> 00:00:42.422
Diagnose the failure before choosing the correction.

00:00:42.422 --> 00:00:47.482
Retrieved but ranked poorly and never retrieved are two different problems.
